"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_4_m_1_d_0_n=self.webpackChunk_4_m_1_d_0_n||[]).push([[3518],{34369:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/retex-insecure-time-token-sandwich-attack","metadata":{"permalink":"/retex-insecure-time-token-sandwich-attack","source":"@site/blog/2025/06/Retex lehack sandwich attack/time_based_attacks_leHack_retex.md","title":"Cracking Time-Based Tokens: A Glimpse from a Workshop During leHACK 2025 Singularity","description":"Experience report from a YesWeHack workshop demonstrating how to exploit a password reset token based on an insecure time-based secret using the open-source tool Reset Tolkien.","date":"2025-06-30T20:44:48.369Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"bug bounty","permalink":"/tags/bug-bounty"},{"inline":true,"label":"pentest","permalink":"/tags/pentest"},{"inline":true,"label":"web security","permalink":"/tags/web-security"},{"inline":true,"label":"reset token","permalink":"/tags/reset-token"},{"inline":true,"label":"YesWeHack","permalink":"/tags/yes-we-hack"},{"inline":true,"label":"retex","permalink":"/tags/retex"},{"inline":true,"label":"automation","permalink":"/tags/automation"},{"inline":true,"label":"appsec","permalink":"/tags/appsec"}],"readingTime":7.795,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Cracking Time-Based Tokens: A Glimpse from a Workshop During leHACK 2025 Singularity","description":"Experience report from a YesWeHack workshop demonstrating how to exploit a password reset token based on an insecure time-based secret using the open-source tool Reset Tolkien.","slug":"/retex-insecure-time-token-sandwich-attack","tags":["bug bounty","pentest","web security","reset token","YesWeHack","retex","automation","appsec"],"hide_table_of_contents":false},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Deploying a  Cybersecurity Lab on VirtualBox","permalink":"/deploy-virtualbox-cyber-lab"}},"content":"On the night of **June 28th, 2025** (technically June 29th, because the workshop was at 3AM \u2014 yes, 3 in the morning), I attended a hands-on workshop at **leHACK 2025 Singularity**, titled:\\r\\n\\r\\n> **\u201cInsecure time-based secret in web applications and Sandwich attack exploitation\u201d**  \\r\\n> by [Tom Chambaretaud](https://www.aeth.cc) \u2014 Technical Lead @YesWeHack & Bug Bounty Hunter\\r\\n\\r\\nThis blog post covers only a small part of the workshop: the exploitation of password reset tokens based on PHP\'s `uniqid()` function.  \\r\\nFor more advanced scenarios and techniques (MongoDB ObjectIDs, rainbow tables, etc.), I highly recommend checking out Tom\u2019s complete write-up:  \\r\\n\ud83d\udc49 **[https://www.aeth.cc](https://www.aeth.cc)**\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n## The Scenario: Predictable Token Generation\\r\\n\\r\\nMany real-world web applications generate password reset tokens using `uniqid()`, a PHP function that creates identifiers based solely on the current timestamp in microseconds, with no added randomness.\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-7.png)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nOfficial PHP documentation for the `uniqid()` function.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen used in token generation, this results in predictable values:\\r\\n\\r\\n![Example of a password reset token generated using `uniqid()`](image-6.png)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nExample of a password reset token generated using `uniqid()`.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\nThis raises the question:  \\r\\n**Can an attacker exploit this by guessing the token generation time and recreating it?**  \\r\\nThe answer: yes.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Attack Flow in Simple Terms\\r\\n\\r\\nHere\'s a simplified version of how an attacker can take advantage of this vulnerability:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. The attacker triggers a password reset for the victim\u2019s email.\\r\\n2. They estimate the time when the server generated the token.\\r\\n3. They reproduce a range of tokens using `uniqid(timestamp)` over a short time window.\\r\\n4. They try each token until one is accepted.\\r\\n\\r\\n![How an attacker can exploit predictable token generation with timing guesses.](token_attack.png)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nHow an attacker can exploit predictable token generation with timing guesses.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n## The Risk of Missing the Target\\r\\n\\r\\nBut what happens if the attacker is just slightly off with their timing?\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-8.png)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nSo close, yet so far. The attacker stops just before hitting the correct token.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](Three-feet-from-gold.jpg)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nSometimes, you\'re just a couple of tokens away from gold.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n## The Sandwich Attack: Bounding the Victim\'s Token Timestamp\\r\\n\\r\\nTo avoid missing the valid token, the attacker must pinpoint and constrain the token\u2019s generation time window. This is where the Sandwich Attack comes in \u2014 a clever way to trap the victim\'s token between two known timestamps.\\r\\n\\r\\n### How It Works\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Send a reset request for an attacker-controlled email \u2192 receive `token\u2081` (known).\\r\\n2. Send a reset request for the victim\u2019s email \u2192 `token\u2082` (unknown).\\r\\n3. Send another reset request for the controlled email \u2192 receive `token\u2083` (known).\\r\\n\\r\\nThis traps the victim\u2019s token `token\u2082` between `token\u2081` and `token\u2083` in time.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Sandwich attack illustrated: victim token is generated between two known ones](sandwich_attack_2.drawio.svg)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nThe victim\u2019s token is guaranteed to have been generated between two attacker-controlled tokens.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\nThe attacker now knows:\\r\\n- `t\u2081`: time of first known token\\r\\n- `t\u2083`: time of third known token  \\r\\n- `t\u2082`: must be between them\\r\\n\\r\\nThis reduces the number of bruteforce attempts significantly.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Narrowing the guess window using the Sandwich Attack](sandwich_attack_3.drawio.svg)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nThe guessing window is now tightly bounded, making bruteforce faster and more effective.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n## Optimizing the Attack: Time Guessing Techniques\\r\\n\\r\\nEven with a bounded range, bruteforce might still take too long \u2014 especially if the tokens are based on microsecond-level timestamps.\\r\\n\\r\\nTo further improve precision, the workshop demonstrated two key techniques.\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. Burp Suite: Sequential or Parallel Requests\\r\\n\\r\\nBy using Burp Suite to send requests very close together in time, either sequentially or in parallel, you can reduce timing noise.\\r\\n\\r\\nAfter intercepting the request in Burp and sending it to Repeater, you can rename the tabs to better understand which request is which.\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-11.png)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nThe first request will give us the first controlled token.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-12.png)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nThe second request will give us the second uncontrolled (victim\'s) token.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-13.png)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nThe third request will give us the third controlled token.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-10.png)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nSending tightly timed reset requests helps reduce uncertainty.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-14.png)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nRight-clicking on one of the tabs, then choosing \\"Create tab group\\".\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-15.png)\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nAfter grouping, we can send the requests in sequence.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\nExample tokens from real testing:\\r\\n\\r\\n- 1st token: `6862d10ad835d`  \\r\\n  ![alt text](image-19.png)\\r\\n  <div class=\\"image_caption\\">First controlled token received by the attacker.</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n- 3rd token: `6862d10ae865f`  \\r\\n  ![alt text](image-21.png)\\r\\n  <div class=\\"image_caption\\">Third controlled token received by the attacker.</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n- Victim\u2019s token: `6862d10addf2a`  \\r\\n  ![alt text](image-20.png)\\r\\n  <div class=\\"image_caption\\">Token received by the victim between two attacker-controlled requests.</div>\\r\\n\\r\\nNow using code adapted from [Tom\'s article](https://www.aeth.cc/public/Article-Reset-Tolkien/secret-time-based-article-fr.html):\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nimport math\\r\\nimport datetime\\r\\n\\r\\ndef uniqid(timestamp: float):\\r\\n    sec = math.floor(timestamp)\\r\\n    usec = round(1000000 * (timestamp - sec))\\r\\n    return \\"%8x%05x\\" % (sec, usec)\\r\\n\\r\\ndef reverse_uniqid(value: str):\\r\\n    return float(\\r\\n        str(int(value[:8], 16))\\r\\n        + \\".\\"\\r\\n        + str(int(value[8:], 16))\\r\\n    )\\r\\n\\r\\ndef check():\\r\\n    t = datetime.datetime.now().timestamp()\\r\\n    u = uniqid(t)\\r\\n    return t == reverse_uniqid(u)\\r\\n\\r\\nif check():\\r\\n    tokens = [\\"68630bcb53e55\\", \\"68630bcb7b2eb\\", \\"68630bcb73f31\\"]\\r\\n    timestamps = [reverse_uniqid(t) for t in tokens]\\r\\n    datetimes = [datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(ts) for ts in timestamps]\\r\\n\\r\\n    for token, ts, dt in zip(tokens, timestamps, datetimes):\\r\\n        print(f\\"{token} - {ts} => {dt}\\")\\r\\n\\r\\n    delta_seconds = abs(timestamps[1] - timestamps[0])\\r\\n    delta_time = abs(datetimes[1] - datetimes[0])\\r\\n    delta_microseconds = delta_seconds * 1_000_000\\r\\n\\r\\n    print(f\\"\\\\nDifference between token 1 and 2:\\")\\r\\n    print(f\\"  {delta_seconds:.6f} seconds\\")\\r\\n    print(f\\"  {delta_microseconds:.0f} microseconds\\")\\r\\n    print(f\\"  {delta_time} (timedelta)\\")\\r\\n\\r\\n    t0, t1, t2 = timestamps\\r\\n    if min(t0, t1) < t2 < max(t0, t1):\\r\\n        print(\\"\\\\nThe third token is between the first and second.\\")\\r\\n    else:\\r\\n        print(\\"\\\\nThe third token is NOT between the first and second.\\")\\r\\nelse:\\r\\n    print(\\"check() failed: reverse_uniqid(uniqid(t)) != t\\")\\r\\n````\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-23.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nThe victim\u2019s token timestamp is confirmed to be between the two attacker-controlled ones.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2. Use the `Date` Header from the Server\\r\\n\\r\\nIn some situations where precise timestamps are hard to determine (or when you don\u2019t have access to reverse-controlled tokens), you can fall back on the HTTP `Date` header. This value is usually added by a proxy or the application server and reflects a stable, server-synced clock.\\r\\n\\r\\nExample:\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nDate: Mon, 30 Jun 2025 18:01:46 GMT\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-25.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nReading the `Date` header to sync with server-side token generation time.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\nThe overall workflow is similar to what we demonstrated earlier: use the timestamp as a base, generate token guesses, and attempt bruteforce.\\r\\n\\r\\nHowever, this technique is more effective when the application uses token generation functions based on **seconds** rather than **microseconds** (e.g., a custom `time()`-based scheme instead of `uniqid()`).  \\r\\nThis is because the `Date` header itself only provides precision up to the **second**, so using it for `uniqid()`-based tokens would result in a wide and inefficient brute-force range.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n## Bonus Tip: Convert Burp Request to Python\\r\\n\\r\\nBurp Suite includes an extension that allows you to copy an HTTP request as a Python script, making it easier to paste on your script to automate the attack flow.\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-26.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nBurp Suite feature to convert requests into Python scripts.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-31.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nRight-click on the request, then choose \\"Copy as requests\\" from the Extensions menu.\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-27.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Reset Tolkien: Automate the Attack\\r\\n\\r\\nTom presented his open-source tool [Reset Tolkien](https://github.com/AethliosIK/reset-tolkien), which automates the full attack chain:\\r\\n\\r\\n* Detection of time-based token generation\\r\\n* Sandwich bounding\\r\\n* Efficient bruteforce across narrow windows\\r\\n\\r\\nYou can test it on a demo app provided by him using Docker (this was tested on Kali Linux):\\r\\n\\r\\n```bash\\r\\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install docker-compose\\r\\nwget https://www.aeth.cc/public/workshop-lehack-2025.tar.gz    \\r\\ntar -xvf workshop-lehack-2025.tar.gz\\r\\ncd workshop-lehack-2025/workshop-step-1\\r\\nsudo docker-compose up --build -d\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image.png)\\r\\n![alt text](image-1.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nVisit [localhost:8000](http://localhost:8000/) to explore:\\r\\n\\r\\n![localhost interface](image-3.png)\\r\\n![levels preview](image-4.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n<div class=\\"image_caption\\">\\r\\nFor this demo, Level 1 will be used (uniqid).\\r\\n</div>\\r\\n\\r\\n![requesting token](image-5.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nFollow the instructions in the GitHub repo to use the tool with each vulnerable scenario.\\r\\n\\r\\n:::note\\r\\nThis article only covers the first scenario, more details on the other scenarios on Tom\'s blog.\\r\\n:::\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nTake the three tokens obtained from Burp Suite and insert them into the Python script. Then use the reset-tolkien tool to generate the token list:\\r\\n\\r\\n```bash\\r\\npython3 script.py\\r\\nreset-tolkien sandwich -bt 1751321547.343637 -et 1751321547.504555 -o output.txt --token-format=\\"uniqid\\" token\\r\\nsort -u output.txt > tokens.txt                                                                                    \\r\\nffuf -w tokens.txt:FUZZ -u \'http://localhost:8000/?level=1&token=FUZZ\' -fs 2886    \\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n![alt text](image-30.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Go Further\\r\\n\\r\\nThis post only scratches the surface of what was covered in the workshop.\\r\\nThere are many other ways timestamps can leak into token generation \u2014 including hashed timestamps, UUIDv1, or even MongoDB ObjectIDs \u2014 each with their own quirks and attack paths.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe tool presented, [Reset Tolkien](https://github.com/AethliosIK/reset-tolkien), is far more powerful than what we covered here.\\r\\nIt supports:\\r\\n\\r\\n* Multiple token formats (including MD5 and SHA-based derivations)\\r\\n* Detection strategies for different token sources\\r\\n* Several real-world examples to train on\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udc49 Check out the full project here: [https://github.com/AethliosIK/reset-tolkien](https://github.com/AethliosIK/reset-tolkien)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Final Thoughts\\r\\n\\r\\nIt was a real pleasure attending this workshop \u2014 I learned a lot about subtle time-based flaws and how they can lead to impactful vulnerabilities like account takeovers.\\r\\n\\r\\nIf you\'re working in AppSec, doing bug bounty, or simply curious about token security, this is definitely a topic worth diving into.\\r\\n\\r\\nBig thanks to [Tom Chambaretaud](https://www.aeth.cc) for the quality of his explanations, the depth of the workshop, and the open-source tooling he shared.\\r\\nAnd hats off to the organizers of [leHACK](https://lehack.org) for making this kind of hands-on training available \u2014 even at 3AM.\\r\\n\\r\\n> Some of the diagrams and phrasing in this article are adapted\u2014or directly screenshotted\u2014from Tom Chambaretaud\'s original documentation and workshop material."},{"id":"deploy-virtualbox-cyber-lab","metadata":{"permalink":"/deploy-virtualbox-cyber-lab","source":"@site/blog/2025/05/2025-05-12-Deploying a Cybersecurity Lab on VirtualBox.mdx","title":"Deploying a  Cybersecurity Lab on VirtualBox","description":"Step-by-step guide to installing and configuring pfSense as the core router/firewall, Splunk as the SIEM in a segmented cybersecurity lab running on VirtualBox.","date":"2025-05-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"cybersecurity","permalink":"/tags/cybersecurity"},{"inline":true,"label":"homelab","permalink":"/tags/homelab"},{"inline":true,"label":"virtualbox","permalink":"/tags/virtualbox"},{"inline":true,"label":"pfSense","permalink":"/tags/pf-sense"},{"inline":true,"label":"network-segmentation","permalink":"/tags/network-segmentation"},{"inline":true,"label":"lab-setup","permalink":"/tags/lab-setup"}],"readingTime":4.28,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Deploying a  Cybersecurity Lab on VirtualBox","description":"Step-by-step guide to installing and configuring pfSense as the core router/firewall, Splunk as the SIEM in a segmented cybersecurity lab running on VirtualBox.","slug":"deploy-virtualbox-cyber-lab","tags":["cybersecurity","homelab","virtualbox","pfSense","network-segmentation","lab-setup"],"hide_table_of_contents":false},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Cracking Time-Based Tokens: A Glimpse from a Workshop During leHACK 2025 Singularity","permalink":"/retex-insecure-time-token-sandwich-attack"}},"content":":::warning\\n**This post is a work in progress \u2014 updates coming soon!** \\n:::\\n\\n\\nThis blog kicks off a series dedicated to building a complete **cybersecurity home lab** on VirtualBox \u2014 designed to support both **red teaming** and **blue teaming** activities. From attacking vulnerable services to detecting malicious behavior with a SIEM, this lab provides a self-contained environment to practice real-world scenarios.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\\nThroughout this project, we deploy and configure:\\n- **Kali Linux**: our attacker and administration machine  \\n- **pfSense**: a firewall/router to segment our lab into realistic network zones  \\n- **GOAD (Active Directory lab)**: to simulate internal corporate infrastructure  \\n- **A vulnerable web server in the DMZ**  \\n- **Splunk** (or ELK): for centralized log collection and detection engineering  \\n- **OpenVAS/Nessus**: to simulate vulnerability scans and management\\n\\nEach system is deployed on VirtualBox and connected through isolated internal networks, allowing realistic simulation of internal, external, and DMZ scenarios. The lab is entirely **offline and open-source friendly** (except where noted, like Nessus Essentials).\\n\\nThis post gives a full architectural overview and deployment philosophy, with links to step-by-step guides for each component.\\n\\n\\nimport React from \'react\';\\n\\n## My Network Architecture\\n\\nHere is the diagram of the VirtualBox network setup:\\n\\n<div style={{position: \\"relative\\", width: \\"100%\\", height: \\"500px\\"}}>\\n  <iframe\\n    className=\\"responsive-iframe\\"\\n    frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n    style={{\\n      position: \\"absolute\\",\\n      top: 0,\\n      left: 0,\\n      width: \\"100%\\",\\n      height: \\"100%\\"\\n    }}\\n    loading=\\"lazy\\"\\n    src=\\"https://viewer.diagrams.net/?tags=%7B%7D&highlight=0000ff&layers=1&nav=1&title=MyDiagram#Uhttps%3A%2F%2Fdrive.google.com%2Fuc%3Fid%3D1dJP_nJqBZliPa_Dum1ZIDOIQa0hSF8CH%26export%3Ddownload\\"\\n  ></iframe>\\n</div>\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n# Building a VirtualBox-Based Cybersecurity Home Lab\\n\\nSetting up a dedicated cybersecurity lab is one of the most effective ways to sharpen your skills, experiment safely, and document your progress. Whether you\'re preparing for certifications, building a red/blue team mindset, or simply exploring new tools, having your own lab environment offers freedom and depth unmatched by online platforms.\\n\\nIn this blog series, I document the complete setup of my **VirtualBox-based cyber range**, focused on **pentesting, blue teaming (SOC), and vulnerability operations (VOC)**. Everything is hosted locally and uses **free, open-source tools**, making it highly accessible and reproducible.\\n\\n> \ud83d\udca1 If you\'re just looking to jump straight into configuring a core component, check out:\\n> [Building a Security Home Lab in VirtualBox](/docs/category/building-a-security-home-lab-in-virtualbox)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Why Build a Home Lab?\\n\\nThere are tons of platforms out there (HTB, TryHackMe, etc.), but building your own lab helps you:\\n\\n* Develop **practical skills** in real-world architectures.\\n* Learn how **network segmentation**, **routing**, and **firewalling** work.\\n* Create controlled environments to test malware, exploits, detections, and defenses.\\n* Practice the **attack\u2013detect\u2013defend** triad used in many professional environments.\\n* Gain hands-on exposure with **SIEM**, **vulnerability scanners**, **Active Directory**, and **DMZ setups**.\\n\\nThis lab is designed to mirror enterprise-grade complexity while staying manageable for a single host machine using VirtualBox.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Lab Architecture Overview\\n\\nHere\'s a high-level view of the lab\u2019s architecture:\\n\\n| Zone                         | Description                                                | Tools/VMs                                   |\\n| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\\n| **Internal Network**      | Simulates a corporate LAN.                                 | GOAD (Active Directory), workstations       |\\n| **DMZ**                   | Exposed web server to simulate public-facing applications. | Vulnerable Linux web app                    |\\n| **Attacker Zone**         | Where attacks are launched and tests are run.              | Kali Linux                                  |\\n| **Routing/Firewall**      | Controls traffic between zones.                            | pfSense                                     |\\n| **SOC (Monitoring)**      | Central log aggregation and detection.                     | ELK Stack (ElasticSearch, Logstash, Kibana) |\\n| **VOC (Vuln Management)** | Detect and manage vulnerabilities.                         | OpenVAS or similar                          |\\n\\nAll virtual machines are interconnected through VirtualBox\'s internal networks, with pfSense acting as the router and firewall between zones. Each network is segmented, offering a realistic testbed for attacks and defenses.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Project Structure\\n\\nThe full deployment is structured in **stages**, with each post documenting:\\n\\n* The **deployment** of a specific component.\\n* Key **configuration steps**.\\n* How each component fits into the bigger picture.\\n* Preparation for a final **Attack / Detect / Defend** demo with CVEs.\\n\\n### Deployment Roadmap:\\n\\n1. Install and configure pfSense with all necessary interfaces\\n2. Install Kali Linux as the attack/admin machine\\n3. Configure pfSense firewall rules\\n4. Deploy a vulnerable Linux web server in the DMZ\\n5. Install GOAD (Active Directory simulation)\\n6. Install and configure the ELK stack for log centralization\\n7. Set up log forwarders and visibility tools\\n8. Deploy OpenVAS for vulnerability scanning\\n9. Launch real-world CVE exploits, detect them via logs, and respond using blue team techniques\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tools and Technologies\\n\\nThis lab uses only **free, open-source** tools:\\n\\n* **VirtualBox** (VMs)\\n* **pfSense** (firewall/router)\\n* **Kali Linux** (attacker)\\n* **GOAD** (Active Directory lab)\\n* **Ubuntu/Debian** (vulnerable apps, web server)\\n* **ELK Stack** (SIEM)\\n* **OpenVAS** (VOC)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Who Is This For?\\n\\nThis series is designed for:\\n\\n* Security enthusiasts wanting to go beyond CTFs.\\n* Pentesters looking to simulate internal/external assessments.\\n* Blue teamers and SOC analysts aiming to improve detection engineering.\\n* Students prepping for certs like OSCP, CPTS, or Blue Team certs.\\n* Anyone building a portfolio to land a first job in cybersecurity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n## Stay Tuned\\n\\nEach new component comes with a full walkthrough and technical write-up. You\u2019ll find detailed steps, explanations, and screenshots.\\n\\nBy the end of this series, you\'ll have a fully functional lab that supports both red and blue team exercises.\\n\\nHappy hacking & defending!"}]}}')}}]);